The first failure, and the name of the root nub

Run 572's panel was the same as the round before it, so the publication
earlier changed nothing -- and it could not have: PE_lockdown_iokit() calls
StartIOKitMatching(), and the publication was in that function all along,
before the sweep it belongs to.

But the panel says more than that.  The name the failing personality wanted
was cpurdebug-interface and the nub was called options-common, and
options-common is the name of the NVRAM service (IONVRAM.cpp), not of the
root nub.  So the failures on the panel are the NVRAM service's matching,
and ours is in another round entirely -- the root nub's, which the panel
never showed because the failure it recorded was the last one, not the
first.

So the failure of the first candidate turned away is kept now, with the
name it wanted and the name the nub has, and the root nub's own name is
written to the panel as well.  The first candidate turned away is ours:
our platform personality carries the highest score of the lot.
